  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nAn upper ridge, still centered over the Arklatex, will continue to \nweaken on Sunday. Despite lowering heights, I would expect there to \nbe less shower and thunderstorm coverage today versus the last \ncouple of days thanks to a lack of forcing mechanism and slightly \nlower low level moisture and PWATs. There will be convection, just \nnot as much. An isolated strong to severe storm and damaging wind \nthreat, or some localized heavy rains, can't be ruled out. But, the \nthreat is quite low and I would expect that we will remain in the \ngeneral thunder area in SPC's Day 1 convective outlook. For Monday, \nexpect a marked increase in convective activity as a weak upper low \nshifts east from the plains into the Ohio valley. Forecast soundings \nsuggest afternoon MLCAPE values will range from around 1,500 J/kg to \nover 2,000 J/kg area wide Monday afternoon. PWATs will increase a \nbit too, all of which will support some isolated damaging wind gusts \nfrom collapsing storms. Storm motions will be slow too, so localized \nheavy rains and flooding could be an issue too. Tuesday should \nfeature fairly widespread showers too, but instability looks far \nless so the severe potential looks negligible.\n\nLastly, despite the waning influence of the ridge, there's no air \nmass change until the mid-week time frame. So temperatures will \nremain well above normal Sunday through Tuesday. Highs in the upper \n80s to mid 90s are on tap for Sun and Mon, with low 80s to low 90s \nfor Tuesday. Barring any unforeseen circumstances, Chattanooga looks \nto continue it's record setting streak of 90 degree days and should \ntop out at 50. Wednesday is the first day of sub-90 forecast highs.\n\nSpeaking of Wednesday, and a break in the heat...late Tuesday into \nWednesday a rex block pattern sets up, with the upper ridge building \non the Canadian side of the Great Lakes, and the upper low \nre-intensifying south of the ridge over the Ohio valley. This will \nresult in surface high pressure well to our north, and northerly low \nlevel flow pushing a cold front through the region sometime on \nWednesday. We'll still be warm...maybe 1-3 degrees above normal as \nopposed to +12-15 degrees...but rain and cloudy conditions will be a \nwelcome change. We'll wrap the week with temperatures around or \nslightly below normal with highs in the 70s to 80s, and lows in the \n50s may even make an appearance by week's end.",
